---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Play Warhammer 40k — Beginner's Guide"
  description="A plain-English guide to how Warhammer 40k actually works. Turn structure, dice mechanics, objectives, and what your first game looks like."
>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 border-b border-[#2a2a35]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <p class="text-[#c9a84c] text-sm font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-4 font-display">How to Play</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mb-6">
        A game of 40k looks<br />overwhelming. It isn't.
      </h1>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        The full rulebook is 60 pages. The core of the game is three dice rolls per attack.
        This guide explains how a game actually works — without drowning you in the full rules before you've played once.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What you're trying to do -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">What you're actually trying to do</h2>
      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 text-lg mb-8">
        <p>
          Most new players assume 40k is about killing all your opponent's models. 
          It isn't. 40k is about <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">controlling objectives</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Objective markers are small tokens placed around the battlefield. 
          Holding them at the right time scores you <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Victory Points (VPs)</strong>. 
          The player with the most VPs at the end of five battle rounds wins. 
          You can table your opponent (kill everything) and still lose 
          if you ignored the objectives while doing it.
        </p>
        <p>
          This matters for how you play: your goal isn't maximum destruction. 
          Your goal is to put models in the right places at the right times 
          while preventing your opponent from doing the same.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#c9a84c] rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-2">The win condition in one sentence</h3>
        <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Score more Victory Points than your opponent over five battle rounds 
          by holding objective markers and completing secondary missions — 
          while keeping enough of your army alive to keep scoring.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- The shape of a game -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">The shape of a game</h2>
      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 text-lg mb-8">
        <p>
          A game is divided into <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Battle Rounds</strong>. 
          There are five of them. In each Battle Round, both players take a turn — 
          the person who goes first finishes their turn, then the other player takes theirs. 
          One Battle Round = both players have acted.
        </p>
        <p>
          Standard games are played at 2,000 points — a full evening's game, 
          typically 2.5 to 3 hours. But you'll start at 
          <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Combat Patrol</strong> (roughly 500 points) — 
          a much smaller game that takes about an hour and uses simplified rules. 
          It's designed exactly for learning.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-3 mb-8">
        {[
          { label: 'Combat Patrol', pts: '~500 pts', time: '~1 hour', note: 'Start here' },
          { label: 'Incursion', pts: '~1000 pts', time: '~1.5 hours', note: 'Next step up' },
          { label: 'Strike Force', pts: '~2000 pts', time: '~2.5–3 hours', note: 'Standard game' },
        ].map(size => (
          <div class={`bg-[#1a1a1f] border rounded-lg p-4 text-center ${size.note === 'Start here' ? 'border-[#c9a84c]' : 'border-[#2a2a35]'}`}>
            <p class="font-display font-semibold text-sm mb-1">{size.label}</p>
            <p class="text-[#c9a84c] text-xs mb-1">{size.pts}</p>
            <p class="text-[#4a4855] text-xs">{size.time}</p>
            {size.note === 'Start here' && <p class="text-[#c9a84c] text-xs font-semibold mt-1">← Start here</p>}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Your turn -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">Your turn — phase by phase</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-10 text-lg">Every turn follows the same sequence. Learn this order and 40k starts making sense.</p>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        {[
          {
            phase: 'Command Phase',
            icon: '⚑',
            detail: 'Rally any units that fled last turn. Use Command Points — a resource that lets you use special abilities called Stratagems throughout the game. Command Points regenerate each turn.',
            key: 'Think of it as your planning phase. Use Stratagems here or save CPs for later.',
          },
          {
            phase: 'Movement Phase',
            icon: '➤',
            detail: 'Move all your units. Each unit has a Movement characteristic (M) — the maximum distance in inches it can move. You can move less, but not more. Units can also Advance (move further but can\'t shoot afterward) or Remain Stationary for bonuses.',
            key: 'This is where 40k is won or lost. Getting units onto objectives early, screening against charges, and cutting off your opponent\'s movement — all happens here.',
          },
          {
            phase: 'Shooting Phase',
            icon: '◎',
            detail: 'All your units with ranged weapons fire at enemy units. Pick a unit, pick a target within range and line of sight, roll dice to hit, roll dice to wound, opponent rolls saves. Remove casualties.',
            key: 'Units that Advanced or are in melee combat cannot shoot. Pick targets carefully — you must declare all shots from a unit before resolving any.',
          },
          {
            phase: 'Charge Phase',
            icon: '⚔',
            detail: 'Declare which units want to charge into melee. Roll 2D6 — if the result equals or exceeds the distance to the target, the charge succeeds and your unit moves into base contact. Opponent can Overwatch (shoot at the charging unit for free).',
            key: 'Failed charges happen. Manage expectations — a 9" charge (two 6s needed) is risky. A 4" charge is almost guaranteed.',
          },
          {
            phase: 'Fight Phase',
            icon: '✦',
            detail: 'Units in melee combat fight. Crucially: BOTH players fight in this phase, not just the active player. The charging player fights first, then the other player. Units locked in combat cannot shoot — getting charged is costly.',
            key: 'This is the most interactive phase. Both players are rolling dice simultaneously (in sequence). Good positioning before the charge determines everything.',
          },
          {
            phase: 'Morale & Battleshock',
            icon: '◈',
            detail: 'Units that took casualties may have to take a Battleshock test. In 10th edition this is simplified — units below half strength are Battleshocked, reducing their effectiveness. No models flee from Battleshock in 10th edition.',
            key: 'Less punishing than previous editions. But Battleshocked units lose their Objective Control bonus — important for scoring.',
          },
        ].map((p, i) => (
          <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
              <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#1f1d14] border border-[#c9a84c] text-[#c9a84c] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">{i + 1}</div>
              <div class="flex-1">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
                  <span class="text-lg">{p.icon}</span>
                  <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-lg">{p.phase}</h3>
                </div>
                <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm leading-relaxed mb-2">{p.detail}</p>
                <p class="text-[#c9a84c] text-xs font-semibold">Key: {p.key}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- The dice -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">How the dice work</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-8 text-lg">
        Every attack in 40k is three rolls: hit, wound, save. 
        That's the whole combat system. Everything else is modifiers on top of these three rolls.
      </p>

      <!-- The three rolls -->
      <div class="space-y-4 mb-10">
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3 text-lg">Roll 1 — To Hit</h3>
          <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed mb-2">
            Roll a D6 for each attack. Compare to the weapon's 
            <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Ballistic Skill</strong> (ranged) or 
            <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Weapon Skill</strong> (melee). 
            Roll equal to or higher than the characteristic to score a hit.
          </p>
          <p class="text-[#4a4855] text-xs">Example: BS 3+ means you need a 3, 4, 5, or 6. Half your rolls hit.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3 text-lg">Roll 2 — To Wound</h3>
          <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
            For each hit, roll again. Compare the weapon's 
            <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Strength (S)</strong> 
            to the target's <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Toughness (T)</strong>:
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 text-xs">
            {[
              { condition: 'S ≥ double T', result: 'Wound on 2+', color: 'text-green-400' },
              { condition: 'S > T', result: 'Wound on 3+', color: 'text-green-400' },
              { condition: 'S = T', result: 'Wound on 4+', color: 'text-yellow-400' },
              { condition: 'S < T', result: 'Wound on 5+', color: 'text-red-400' },
              { condition: 'T ≥ double S', result: 'Wound on 6+', color: 'text-red-400' },
            ].map(row => (
              <div class="bg-[#111113] rounded px-3 py-2">
                <span class="text-[#8a8590]">{row.condition} → </span>
                <span class={`font-semibold ${row.color}`}>{row.result}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3 text-lg">Roll 3 — Armor Save</h3>
          <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed mb-2">
            The <em>defending</em> player rolls a D6 for each wound. 
            Compare to the model's <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Save</strong> characteristic. 
            Roll equal to or higher to save the wound — no damage taken. 
            Fail the save — remove a wound (or the model if wounds reach zero).
          </p>
          <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Weapons have an <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Armour Penetration (AP)</strong> value 
            that worsens the target's save. 
            AP -1 means a 3+ save becomes a 4+. AP -3 means a 3+ save becomes a 6+.
          </p>
          <p class="text-[#4a4855] text-xs mt-2">Example: Space Marine saves on 3+. Hit by a lascannon (AP -3). Now saves on 6+. Most shots get through.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Keywords -->
      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#c9a84c] rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-2">The one rule that confuses everyone: Invulnerable Saves</h3>
        <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Some models have an <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Invulnerable Save</strong> — 
          a second save that ignores AP entirely. 
          A model with a 5++ (invulnerable) always saves on 5+ regardless of the weapon's AP. 
          The defending player uses whichever save is better. 
          Invulnerable saves make certain models extremely hard to kill with high-AP weapons.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Key concepts -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-8">Key concepts you'll encounter immediately</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        {[
          {
            concept: 'Line of Sight',
            detail: 'A unit can shoot at a target if it can draw a line from any model in the unit to any part of the target unit. In practice: look from roughly model eye level. If you can see the target, you can shoot it. Terrain blocks line of sight completely (area terrain) or partially (ruins).',
          },
          {
            concept: 'Cover',
            detail: 'Models partially behind terrain get a cover bonus — typically +1 to their armor save. Makes units harder to kill when positioned behind walls, ruins, and obstacles. Always use terrain. Always.',
          },
          {
            concept: 'Objective Control (OC)',
            detail: 'Each model has an OC value — usually 1 or 2. Add up the OC of all your models on an objective marker. If your total is higher than your opponent\'s total, you control it. Battleshocked units contribute 0 OC.',
          },
          {
            concept: 'Unit Coherency',
            detail: 'Models in a unit must stay within 2" of at least one other model in the same unit. If a unit gets spread out, you have to move models back into coherency at the end of your movement phase. Spreading out means less area controlled.',
          },
          {
            concept: 'Keywords',
            detail: 'Every unit has a list of keywords — tags that abilities and rules reference. INFANTRY, VEHICLE, CHARACTER, FLY, MONSTER, CHAOS, IMPERIUM, etc. Many rules say "applies to INFANTRY units" or "cannot target CHARACTERs unless..." — knowing your keywords matters.',
          },
          {
            concept: 'Characters and Leader',
            detail: 'Characters can attach to infantry units as Leaders, granting the unit special abilities. A character attached to a unit fights with it, shoots with it, and provides its buff as long as it\'s alive. Characters can\'t be targeted while attached — they\'re protected by the squad.',
          },
          {
            concept: 'Stratagems',
            detail: 'Special abilities you activate by spending Command Points (CPs). Each faction has unique Stratagems — some let units fight again, some negate wounds, some give extra attacks. CPs regenerate each turn. Learning your Stratagems is a big part of improving at the game.',
          },
        ].map(item => (
          <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
            <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-2 text-[#f0ede8]">{item.concept}</h3>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm leading-relaxed">{item.detail}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Building your army -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">Building your first army list</h2>
      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 text-lg mb-8">
        <p>
          Every model has a <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">points cost</strong>. 
          An army list is a selection of units within an agreed points limit. 
          You build your list, your opponent builds theirs to the same limit, 
          and that's what you bring to the table.
        </p>
        <p>
          For your first games: use the <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Combat Patrol box straight out of the box</strong>. 
          It's already balanced as a list. You don't need to think about list building 
          until you've played five or ten games and understand what your units actually do.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        {[
          {
            title: 'Detachment',
            desc: 'In 10th edition, you choose a Detachment for your army — a pre-built army structure that gives you a set of Enhancements, Stratagems, and a Detachment Rule. Each faction has several options. Pick the one that sounds most fun, not the one that sounds most powerful.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Points cost',
            desc: 'Available free from Warhammer Community — look up your faction\'s points document. Each unit has a base cost. Most weapons and upgrades are included in that cost in 10th edition, which simplified things significantly.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Battle size determines point limit',
            desc: 'Combat Patrol: 500 pts. Incursion: 1,000 pts. Strike Force: 2,000 pts. Start at Combat Patrol and only move up when you feel confident with the game flow at that size.',
          },
          {
            title: 'Battlescribe or Warhammer app',
            desc: 'Free army list builder apps that do the points math for you. Battlescribe is community-maintained and always current. The official Warhammer 40k app is GW\'s version. Both work. Use one from the beginning.',
          },
        ].map(item => (
          <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1 text-[#f0ede8]">{item.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm leading-relaxed">{item.desc}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Your first game -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-8">Your first game — what to actually focus on</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4 mb-10">
        {[
          {
            priority: 'Learn the turn sequence, not the rules',
            detail: 'Your first game should be about getting through Command → Movement → Shooting → Charge → Fight → Morale in the right order without forgetting a phase. The specific rules of each phase come with repetition.',
          },
          {
            priority: 'Ask questions out loud',
            detail: 'Tell your opponent what you\'re trying to do before you do it. "I want to move this squad here and shoot at that unit — is that legal?" Most players are happy to help beginners. The community is generally welcoming.',
          },
          {
            priority: 'Focus on objectives early',
            detail: 'In the first two battle rounds, get units onto objective markers. Don\'t get distracted by killing things that aren\'t threatening your objectives. Holding objectives from turn 1 builds a scoring lead that\'s hard to overcome.',
          },
          {
            priority: 'Don\'t worry about winning',
            detail: 'Your first game is a tutorial. You will make mistakes, forget phases, and probably lose. That\'s fine. Every veteran player has a story about their first terrible game. The goal is to understand what happened and why — not to win.',
          },
          {
            priority: 'Play one more game immediately',
            detail: 'The second game, armed with what you learned from the first, is dramatically better. Book your second game before you leave the first.',
          },
        ].map((item, i) => (
          <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-[#c9a84c] font-display font-bold text-sm flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5">{i + 1}.</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">{item.priority}</p>
                <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm leading-relaxed">{item.detail}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <!-- Where to play -->
      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3">Where to find games</h3>
        <div class="space-y-2 text-sm text-[#b8b4c0]">
          <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Local game store (LGS):</strong> The best place to start. Most stores with a 40k section have a gaming room and regular play nights. Walk in and ask — staff will introduce you to the community.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Warhammer stores:</strong> GW's own retail stores run demo games and beginner events. Free to attend, often include a free model. Look up your nearest store at games-workshop.com.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Warhammer Community finder:</strong> Games Workshop's official store and event finder at warhammer-community.com includes club finders for your region.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Reddit & Facebook:</strong> r/Warhammer40k, r/40kLore, faction-specific subreddits, and local Facebook groups. People post Looking For Game (LFG) threads constantly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Free resources -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">Free resources to learn the rules</h2>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        {[
          {
            name: 'Warhammer Community — Free Core Rules',
            url: 'https://www.warhammer-community.com/en-gb/articles/warhammer-40000-rules/',
            desc: 'The full core rules PDF is free to download from Games Workshop\'s website. 60 pages, but the first 30 are what you actually need to play.',
          },
          {
            name: 'Battlescribe',
            url: 'https://battlescribe.net/',
            desc: 'Free army list builder. Has every faction, all points costs, and validates your list automatically. Essential.',
          },
          {
            name: 'Goonhammer — Start Competing guides',
            url: 'https://www.goonhammer.com/',
            desc: 'The best tactical writing on the internet for 40k. Their faction guides and "How to Play" articles are detailed, funny, and genuinely helpful.',
          },
          {
            name: 'Auspex Tactics on YouTube',
            url: 'https://www.youtube.com/@AuspexTactics',
            desc: 'Prolific YouTube channel covering rules breakdowns, faction analysis, and news. Good for understanding how units actually work in practice.',
          },
          {
            name: 'Tabletop Battles app',
            url: 'https://www.tabletopbattles.app/',
            desc: 'Tracks game score, missions, and tactical objectives during a game. Makes your first games much easier to manage without paper.',
          },
        ].map(res => (
          <a
            href={res.url}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="block bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] hover:border-[#c9a84c] rounded-lg p-5 transition-colors group"
          >
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1 group-hover:text-[#c9a84c] transition-colors">{res.name} →</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm leading-relaxed">{res.desc}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="px-6 py-20 text-center">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-4">You know enough to play.</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-lg mb-8">
        The rules make more sense after one game than after hours of reading. 
        Get your Combat Patrol built and painted, find an opponent, 
        and learn the rest at the table.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
        <a
          href="/factions"
          class="inline-block bg-[#c9a84c] hover:bg-[#dfc070] text-[#111113] font-semibold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg transition-colors font-display tracking-wide"
        >
          Find Your Faction →
        </a>
        <a
          href="/quiz"
          class="inline-block border border-[#2a2a35] hover:border-[#c9a84c] text-[#b8b4c0] hover:text-[#c9a84c] font-semibold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg transition-colors font-display"
        >
          Take the Army Quiz
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
